      },
      "worked": "Let $t=\\sin x+\\cos x$. Using $\\sin^2 x+\\cos^2 x=1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}t^2&=1+2\\sin x\\cos x\\\\2\\sin x\\cos x&=t^2-1.\\end{align*}$$\nThe equation becomes\n$$\\begin{align*}t^2-1&=2t-1\\\\t^2-2t&=0\\\\t(t-2)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $t=0$ or $t=2$. Since $2\\sin x\\cos x\\le\\sin^2 x+\\cos^2 x=1$, we have $t^2\\le2$, so $t=2$ is impossible.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin x+\\cos x&=0\\\\\\sin x&=-\\cos x\\\\\\tan x&=-1.\\end{align*}$$\nDivision by $\\cos x$ is valid here: $\\cos x=0$ would require $\\sin x=0$ as well. The related angle is $\\frac\\pi4$, with tangent negative in quadrants 2 and 4.\n$$x=\\frac{3\\pi}{4},\\ \\frac{7\\pi}{4}.$$\nFor each value, $\\sin x+\\cos x=0$ and $2\\sin x\\cos x=-1$, so both sides of the original equation equal $-1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
